an society  was different from the anglo-indian the  second part that between cultures points  out the fact that ago ins have always  lived among various cultures with a  mixture of cultures and that this  condition which one finds throughout the  Portuguese world is an integral part of  globalization of today so I wanted to  point out how the example of core the  case of Goya predicted to a certain  extent and could help inform our views  and studies of globalization okay and  who were some of the people who came to  the conference so I was able to contact  several authors before I have a Yale  seminar I normally give called world  cities and narratives and for the book  on Goa I had used the novel titled  tbling by Victor anchovy beta and mr.  anchovy beta got in touch with me he  actually lives in the United States and  helped to organize the conference and so  through him I was able to invite dr.  Antonio Gomez a new novelist a  cardiologist at Mount Sinai Hospital ago  and has been in New York for 40 years  who's now written a very interesting  novel and also I was able to invite  Margaret muscadine yes a best-selling  author  the penguin edition of skin her novel  about go of visited by various  nationalities and people from different  parts of the world worked out so I was  able to have a roundtable with readings  and discussion with the three novelist  Victor Angela rebated Margaret  mysterious and Tony Gomez I'm interested  in the cardiologist what what is his  novel about so his novel called the  sting of peppercorns is said in 1961  when Portuguese rule in Goa ended and  India was incorporated into I'm ago I  was incorporated into India  so his novel really is about that moment  of invasion incorporation liberation and  so forth in December of 1961 okay let's  talk about some of the highlights of the  conference yes in 2006 I had organized a  conference called Portuguese world music  and there were some go and content in  that conference and professor Victor  Coelho from Boston University who's a  prominent  at that time played and he suggested a  concert for this concert of religious  music that was taken to Goa in the 17th  century and this would be Italian sacred  music so he played the Thera bow which  is a very long necked lute and  accompanied a male soprano which is  something that's one almost never hears  playing music of Carissimi and caps  Berger two composers of the 17th century  and this was in Whitechapel and with the  acoustics and the soaring voices it was  really splendid I think that was  certainly one of the highlights of the  conference ok and what did you have any  did you encounter any surprises  something that you didn't anticipate  during the conference well there was an  interesting sideline when one of the  speakers began talking about his going  topic then dr. Gomez stood up and said I  think we're cousins so many of the girls  discovered that they were related and  that was sort of the sub theme that all  Goines throughout the world have some  degree of kinship it's just a matter of  discovering what the context is with the  relative and that became a kind of  humorous under theme that that United  the members of the conference and gave  us all something more to talk about so  why do you think it's important to do  conferences like this well in this case  of course we have a Council of South  Asian Studies but within that account so  there's so many topics that could be  presented for conferences the idea of  doing one on a small territory is the  smallest state in India one that also  for hundreds of years  was ruled by a colonial power might not  be the first choice so I thought that  coming from my background having studied  a bit of ethnography Portuguese studies  literature and go that that would be an  angle that no one else in South Asian  Studies might have and this would be a  chance then to look at a different part  of India of one that's not so perhaps so  traditionally India but that mixes  Eastern Western themes over the years  yeah it is interesting because I  normally don't think of Portuguese when  I think of India I think of of course  England  so that is an interesting aspect so what  can we take away from the conference  what were some of the conclusions that  came out of it well I think we can take  away first of all that Goa is a dynamic  society undergoing tremendous change  so with this globalization we have the  NGO and diaspora that was one of the sub  teams that became prominent in the  conference and perhaps something that we  really learned that the Diaspora of the  gun population has been going on for at  least several hundred years particularly  in East Africa there were Goins who work  for the Portuguese civil service all  over the world and the Portuguese Empire  we had a even a presentation on Goins in  Brazil the places in the world that have  the greatest go in populations Toronto  and New York area other parts of the  world in Europe and so forth and  Australia of course many guns so the  whole idea of a global connection of  Goines society the returning to the  ancestor place the memories of what the  culture was like in the past these all  become prominent themes for people who  feel they're going identity but who  reside in various parts of the world  very good thank you so much for being  here with us today and sharing some of  your work thank you it's a pleasure for  more information about Professor Jackson  and his work please visit our website at  Yale ddu slash McMillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the McMillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty McMillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale  you
 

